Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `id` (ghcnd_stations) : unique identifier of every weather station – we will return it and use it to join with other tables later.  
• `name` (ghcnd_stations) : descriptive station name – we will return it.  
• `latitude` / `longitude` (ghcnd_stations) : coordinates used to build GEOGRAPHY points and compute straight–line (great‑circle) distance to Chicago’s centre.  
• `state` (ghcnd_stations) : 2–letter state code – lets us know in which state the station sits and can be joined to `ghcnd_states` for the full state name.  
• `code` / `name` (ghcnd_states) : state code / state full name – used only when we want the state’s full name.  

Typical filter / join conditions that may be used  
• `ST_DISTANCE( ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude) , ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847 , 41.8319) ) <= 50000`  – stations within 50 km (50 000 m).  
• `LEFT(s.id,2) = st.code`  – if you prefer to map by the first two characters of the station id (often the country code) instead of using the `state` column.  
• `s.state = st.code` – direct state join.  

Ten exploratory → increasingly complex queries  
```sql
--Description: 1) Inspect a handful of rows in the stations table.
SELECT id, name, latitude, longitude, state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) List distinct state codes that appear in the station file (first 20).
SELECT DISTINCT state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE state IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) See stations whose names already contain the word “CHICAGO”.
SELECT id, name, latitude, longitude
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE LOWER(name) LIKE '%chicago%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Compute raw distance (metres) for those “Chicago” stations to check formula.
SELECT id,
       name,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE LOWER(name) LIKE '%chicago%'
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) First attempt at filtering ≤ 50 km; show id, name and distance.
SELECT id,
       name,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Same filter but order by nearest‑to‑farthest for easy inspection.
SELECT id,
       name,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Add the station’s 2‑letter state code and full state name (join to states table).
SELECT s.id,
       s.name,
       s.state AS state_cd,
       st.name AS state_full,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations` AS s
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_states`      AS st
       ON s.state = st.code
WHERE ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Check how many stations are in range and have daily temperature maxima (TMAX) in the inventory.
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT s.id) AS station_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`  AS s
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_inventory` AS i
  ON s.id = i.id AND i.element = 'TMAX'
WHERE ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) List those TMAX‑reporting stations within 50 km, with first & last year of records.
SELECT s.id,
       s.name,
       MIN(i.firstyear) AS firstyr,
       MAX(i.lastyear)  AS lastyr,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`  AS s
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_inventory` AS i
  ON s.id = i.id
WHERE i.element = 'TMAX'
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
GROUP BY s.id, s.name, dist_m
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
